window.docContents[102].resolve({"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus--Boundary-Calculus-and-C___-Boundary-Geometry--Tangential-coordinate-changes":{"contents":"For two charts, define the overlap parameter domainThe tangential coordinate transition is\n\nOpenness makes the overlap measurable and regularity supplies a derivative.\nTogether with injectivity, these are the hypotheses of the same-dimensional\nchange-of-variables theorem applied to .\n\nThe overlap  is open, and\n is globally .\n\nThe overlap is the intersection of the open parameter domain with the\npreimage of the open source of chart  under the continuous map .\nThe transition is a composition of , chart , and the\ncontinuous linear flat projection, all globally .\n\nThe transition is designed so that both charts parametrize the same physical\npoint. Verifying this also shows that  lands in the parameter\ndomain of chart , because a frontier point has zero height in every chart\ncontaining it.\n\nFor ,and\n\nThe point  lies on the physical frontier. Its image under chart\n therefore has last coordinate zero. Re-embedding its tangential\nprojection recovers the full flattened point. The chart right-inverse\nidentity then gives the parametrization equality.\n\nSince  is injective on , the identity\n determines  uniquely, and the\ntransition in the opposite direction must undo it. Injectivity of the\ntransition and the exact identification of its image are then formal\nconsequences.\n\nThe transition sends  into , and the\nreverse transition is its inverse there:Consequently,  is injective on  and\n\nBoth  and  parametrize the same physical\nboundary point through . Injectivity of  on its parameter\ndomain proves the inverse identity. That identity gives injectivity.\nApplying the same result with  and  exchanged gives surjectivity onto\nthe reverse overlap.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Coordinates and Boundary Calculus\u0009Boundary Calculus and C¹ Boundary Geometry","header":"3.3.2.8. Tangential coordinate changes","id":"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus--Boundary-Calculus-and-C___-Boundary-Geometry--Tangential-coordinate-changes"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Using-Verified-Computation":{"contents":"We can first inspect a calculation, then use a tactic to prove a statement\nabout it. #cas prints the input, computed result, available checked evidence,\nand planner metadata in the InfoView. It adds auxiliary theorems to check the\ndisplayed proof evidence in Lean's kernel; it does not close a user's theorem\ngoal. Representative commands use the same syntax throughout:\n\n-- Exact algebra and calculus.\n#cas [derive x] (2 * x)\n#cas [simplify] ((x + 0) * 1)\n#cas [derive] (sin x)\n#cas [derive x] (sin (x * y))\n#cas [derive x] (x ^ (3 / 2 : ℝ))\n#cas [grad x, y] (sin (x * y))\n#cas [laplacian x, y] (x ^ 2 + y ^ 2)\n#cas [integrate] (sin x)\n#cas [integrate x] (y * exp x)\n\n-- Certified ranges, with the upper, absolute and midpoint bounds read off them.\n#cas [enclose x in [-1 / 10, 1 / 10]] (cos x)\n#cas [enclose x in [0, 1]] (x - 2)\n\n-- Refine an enclosure with a higher approximation degree or subdivision.\n#cas (prec := 40) [enclose x in [0, 1]] (exp x)\n#cas (depth := 3) [enclose x in [0, 2]] (sin x * cos x)\n\n\nThe partial derivative names its coordinate; grad and laplacian name an\nordered list. The real-power calculation records the base-positivity condition.\nenclose reports both endpoints of a certified interval and reads three bounds\noff it: an upper bound, a magnitude bound, and a midpoint with its radius.\nPrecision and subdivision settings are explained with the enclosure algorithms\nbelow.\n\nBare derive and bare integrate use a function's binder when given a unary lambda,\nor the sole free variable of a bare expression. Thus\n#cas [derive] (fun x : ℝ ↦ 2 * x) returns 2, and\n#cas [derive] (fun x ↦ a * x + b) returns a with a and b as parameters.\nThe binder also allows constant bodies: #cas [integrate] (fun x ↦ 2) returns 2*x.\nA named coordinate must agree with the binder. Multiplication uses *, and these\ncommands need no prior variable declarations or #eval.\n\nExact differentiation is finite-dimensional and supports any finite list of\ncoordinates. The numerical enclose command accepts any finite list of named\ncoordinates with exact rational interval endpoints. Every free variable must\nhave a box binding. An optional configuration group before the mode bracket,\n(prec := n) or (depth := n), selects the precision policy described in the\nenclosure section; the command and the cega tactic accept it alike.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation","header":"10.5. Using Verified Computation","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Using-Verified-Computation"}});